\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.6}{\ignorespaces Supplementary pooling comparison using seed44 fivefold probability ensembles. ForecastMean and ForecastAttention share forecast readout and future-state supervision, differing in spatial pooling. Probabilities are averaged before metrics; Macro averages the three landmarks.}}{19}{table.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:pooling}{{A.6}{19}{Supplementary pooling comparison using seed44 fivefold probability ensembles. ForecastMean and ForecastAttention share forecast readout and future-state supervision, differing in spatial pooling. Probabilities are averaged before metrics; Macro averages the three landmarks}{table.6}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.7}{\ignorespaces All 14 macro metrics for the principal variants: point estimate [95\% patient-bootstrap percentile interval]. Each patient\textquotesingle s five seed44 fold-model probabilities are averaged first; metrics are then computed and equally averaged over T0--T2. Threshold-dependent metrics use 0.5. Identical patient resamples preserve pairing across models and landmarks. Entries are marginal model intervals; paired differences are reported separately. Intervals condition on the fitted ensembles and exclude retraining and retrospective selection uncertainty. Downward arrows identify metrics for which lower is better.}}{19}{table.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:extended_macro}{{A.7}{19}{All 14 macro metrics for the principal variants: point estimate [95\% patient-bootstrap percentile interval]. Each patient\textquotesingle s five seed44 fold-model probabilities are averaged first; metrics are then computed and equally averaged over T0--T2. Threshold-dependent metrics use 0.5. Identical patient resamples preserve pairing across models and landmarks. Entries are marginal model intervals; paired differences are reported separately. Intervals condition on the fitted ensembles and exclude retraining and retrospective selection uncertainty. Downward arrows identify metrics for which lower is better}{table.7}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.8}{\ignorespaces Operating-point sensitivity analysis for seed-44 test ensemble scores, shown as three-landmark metric means. The alternative threshold maximizes Youden index on concatenated, disjoint out-of-fold development predictions, separately at each landmark; the largest threshold breaks ties. Each development patient contributes one score from a model that did not train on that patient. OOF scores arise from single fold models, whereas test scores average five model probabilities: this distribution mismatch makes this a sensitivity analysis, not validated ensemble calibration. No test labels select thresholds.}}{19}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:extended_threshold}{{A.8}{19}{Operating-point sensitivity analysis for seed-44 test ensemble scores, shown as three-landmark metric means. The alternative threshold maximizes Youden index on concatenated, disjoint out-of-fold development predictions, separately at each landmark; the largest threshold breaks ties. Each development patient contributes one score from a model that did not train on that patient. OOF scores arise from single fold models, whereas test scores average five model probabilities: this distribution mismatch makes this a sensitivity analysis, not validated ensemble calibration. No test labels select thresholds}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.9}{\ignorespaces Exploratory landmark comparisons. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles.}}{20}{table.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:significance_landmarks}{{A.9}{20}{Exploratory landmark comparisons. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles}{table.9}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.10}{\ignorespaces Exploratory Macro operating and probability-quality comparisons. Differences are first-named minus second-named model; negative Brier, NLL, or ECE differences favor the first model. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles.}}{21}{table.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:significance_extended}{{A.10}{21}{Exploratory Macro operating and probability-quality comparisons. Differences are first-named minus second-named model; negative Brier, NLL, or ECE differences favor the first model. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles}{table.10}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.11}{\ignorespaces Exploratory pooling comparisons: ForecastAttention minus ForecastMean. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles.}}{21}{table.11}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:significance_pooling}{{A.11}{21}{Exploratory pooling comparisons: ForecastAttention minus ForecastMean. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles}{table.11}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {A.12}{\ignorespaces Exploratory paired T2 minus T0 comparisons. DeLong placements and bootstrap draws retain within-patient dependence between landmarks. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm  {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles.}}{22}{table.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:significance_longitudinal}{{A.12}{22}{Exploratory paired T2 minus T0 comparisons. DeLong placements and bootstrap draws retain within-patient dependence between landmarks. AUROC uses paired DeLong tests; other metrics use approximate, null-centered paired patient-bootstrap tests (99,999 shared resamples). All $p$ values are two-sided. $p_{\mathrm {Holm}}$ adjusts jointly over the 81 secondary tests in Tables~\ref {tab:significance_landmarks}--\ref {tab:significance_longitudinal}. Direct and Attention abbreviate DirectReadout and ForecastAttention. $^{*}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.05$, $^{**}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.01$, $^{***}p_{\mathrm {Holm}}<0.001$; ns, not significant after the stated correction. Intervals are unadjusted 95\% patient-bootstrap intervals (2,000 shared draws) and need not agree with adjusted test decisions. All analyses condition on the fitted, retrospectively selected seed44 ensembles}{table.12}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A.7}{\ignorespaces Six complementary landmark summaries: AUROC, AP, F1, sensitivity, specificity, and Brier score. All points are calculated from the seed-44 five-fold ensemble probabilities; shading is the conditional 95\% patient-bootstrap interval. The same 175 patients appear at every landmark. A trend in ranking can coexist with a different trend in calibration or error types.}}{22}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:longitudinal}{{A.7}{22}{Six complementary landmark summaries: AUROC, AP, F1, sensitivity, specificity, and Brier score. All points are calculated from the seed-44 five-fold ensemble probabilities; shading is the conditional 95\% patient-bootstrap interval. The same 175 patients appear at every landmark. A trend in ranking can coexist with a different trend in calibration or error types}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A.8}{\ignorespaces Outcome-conditioned changes in predicted pCR probability from T0 to T2. Panels separate models and outcome classes. Histograms are calculated directly from the seed-44 five-fold ensemble probability changes for each patient. Changes describe model scores and do not establish an individual treatment effect.}}{23}{figure.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:scorechanges}{{A.8}{23}{Outcome-conditioned changes in predicted pCR probability from T0 to T2. Panels separate models and outcome classes. Histograms are calculated directly from the seed-44 five-fold ensemble probability changes for each patient. Changes describe model scores and do not establish an individual treatment effect}{figure.8}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {B.13}{\ignorespaces Exploratory subgroup discrimination for the seed44 fivefold probability ensemble. Each patient\textquotesingle s probabilities are averaged over five fold models before computing AUROC; Macro then averages T0--T2 AUROCs. Intervals are paired patient-bootstrap intervals for ForecastAttention minus NoJEPA, conditional on the fitted ensemble. Age 50 is an exploratory reporting cutpoint. Namespace groups are parts of one clinical release, not external cohorts.}}{23}{table.13}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:subgroups}{{B.13}{23}{Exploratory subgroup discrimination for the seed44 fivefold probability ensemble. Each patient\textquotesingle s probabilities are averaged over five fold models before computing AUROC; Macro then averages T0--T2 AUROCs. Intervals are paired patient-bootstrap intervals for ForecastAttention minus NoJEPA, conditional on the fitted ensemble. Age 50 is an exploratory reporting cutpoint. Namespace groups are parts of one clinical release, not external cohorts}{table.13}{}}
\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {B.14}{\ignorespaces Archived seed44 epoch 20 fixed-training-probe diagnostics. These are descriptive means of five fold-specific skills, distinct from probability ensembling for pCR. Skill is $1-\mathrm  {L1}_{forecast}/\mathrm  {L1}_{copy}$ within each snapshot\textquotesingle s EMA coordinates; positive is better than holding the landmark state constant. Forecast targets require a continuous actually observed visit chain. These are training-probe summaries, not best-checkpoint holdout forecast evaluations. NoJEPA has no trained-world diagnostic.}}{23}{table.14}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:worldprobe}{{B.14}{23}{Archived seed44 epoch 20 fixed-training-probe diagnostics. These are descriptive means of five fold-specific skills, distinct from probability ensembling for pCR. Skill is $1-\mathrm {L1}_{forecast}/\mathrm {L1}_{copy}$ within each snapshot\textquotesingle s EMA coordinates; positive is better than holding the landmark state constant. Forecast targets require a continuous actually observed visit chain. These are training-probe summaries, not best-checkpoint holdout forecast evaluations. NoJEPA has no trained-world diagnostic}{table.14}{}}
